# Hidden interview questions, Lessons 11–17. Same format as interview_a.Q
Q = [
 (11, 1, "What does a kNN model learn during training?",
  "Describing weights or a boundary.",
  "Nothing beyond storing the training data (and perhaps building a search index). It's a \"lazy\" learner: all the work happens at prediction time, which is why predictions get slow on large datasets and why deploying it means deploying the data."),
 (11, 2, "With $k = 1$, what's the training accuracy, and what does it tell you?",
  "Reporting it as evidence the model is good.",
  "Essentially 100%: each training point's nearest neighbour is itself (unless identical points have different labels). It tells you nothing about generalisation — always evaluate kNN on held-out data."),
 (11, 1, "Why is an odd $k$ often recommended for two-class kNN?",
  "Not noticing ties.",
  "With an even $k$ the vote can tie (2 vs 2). An odd $k$ avoids ties for two classes; with more classes ties can still happen, so implementations break them by distance or order."),
 (11, 2, "Your kNN accuracy jumps after you standardise the features. Why?",
  "\"Scaling just helps.\"",
  "Distances are dominated by features with large numeric ranges. In Lesson 11, income in units of thousands drowned out age entirely. Standardising lets every feature contribute in proportion to how much it normally varies."),
 (11, 3, "Why does kNN struggle with hundreds of features even when you have lots of data?",
  "\"It just gets slower.\"",
  "The curse of dimensionality: in high dimensions, the nearest and farthest neighbours end up at similar distances, so \"nearest\" stops meaning \"similar\". A neighbourhood holding 10% of the data must span about 79% of each feature's range in 10 dimensions. Reduce dimensions or select features first."),
 (11, 2, "How would you use kNN for regression?",
  "Thinking kNN only classifies.",
  "Predict the average (or distance-weighted average) of the $k$ neighbours' target values. It can't predict outside the range of the training targets."),

 (12, 2, "Why is Naive Bayes called \"naive\", and why does it still work?",
  "Thinking the assumption must hold for it to work.",
  "It assumes features are independent given the class, which is rarely true (words like \"free\" and \"prize\" go together). It still classifies well because it only needs the <em>right class to score highest</em>, not accurate probabilities — the ranking often survives the wrong assumption."),
 (12, 2, "Can you trust Naive Bayes probabilities?",
  "Reporting them as calibrated.",
  "Usually not. Counting correlated evidence several times pushes probabilities towards 0 or 1 — in Lesson 12, repeating \"free\" lifted the spam probability from 96% to 99.2%. Use it for ranking or decisions, and calibrate if you need probabilities."),
 (12, 1, "A word never appeared in any ham email during training. What happens without smoothing?",
  "Thinking one word can't matter much.",
  "Its estimated probability in ham is 0, so the whole ham score is 0 and the email is classified as spam with certainty, whatever its other words say. Laplace (add-one) smoothing prevents this."),
 (12, 2, "Why do implementations add log-probabilities instead of multiplying probabilities?",
  "\"It's just a convention.\"",
  "Multiplying hundreds of small numbers underflows to 0 in floating point. Logs turn the product into a sum of moderate numbers, and the class ranking is unchanged because log is increasing."),
 (12, 3, "Is Naive Bayes a generative or a discriminative model? Why does it matter?",
  "Not knowing the distinction.",
  "Generative: it models how the features are produced in each class, $P(\\mathbf{x} \\mid y)P(y)$, then uses Bayes' theorem. Discriminative models (logistic regression) model $P(y \\mid \\mathbf{x})$ directly. Generative models can do well with little data; discriminative ones usually win with more."),
 (12, 1, "Which Naive Bayes variant would you use for word counts, yes/no features, and measurements?",
  "Using GaussianNB for everything.",
  "Multinomial for counts (word frequencies), Bernoulli for binary presence/absence, Gaussian for continuous measurements such as weights or lengths."),

 (13, 1, "Do decision trees need feature scaling?",
  "Scaling everything by habit.",
  "No. A split asks \"is $x \\le t$?\", and rescaling $x$ just rescales $t$; the tree is identical. The same goes for random forests and gradient-boosted trees."),
 (13, 2, "A regression tree is trained on $x = 0, \\dots, 9$ with $y = 2x$. What does it predict for $x = 100$?",
  "Expecting 200.",
  "18 — the value of the last leaf. Trees predict constants inside regions, so they can't extrapolate beyond the training range. Random forests and boosted trees share this limitation; linear models don't."),
 (13, 2, "Why do fully grown decision trees overfit?",
  "\"Trees are just bad.\"",
  "Without limits a tree keeps splitting until every leaf is pure, often isolating single noisy examples. Limit depth or leaf size, prune, or average many trees (a random forest)."),
 (13, 2, "Gini or entropy — does the choice matter much?",
  "Spending a long time tuning it.",
  "Rarely. They rank splits almost identically (in Lesson 13's example both preferred split A). Gini is slightly faster to compute. Depth and leaf-size limits matter far more."),
 (13, 3, "Why can impurity-based feature importance be misleading?",
  "Reading <code>feature_importances_</code> as the truth.",
  "It's computed on training data and favours features with many possible split points (continuous or high-cardinality, like an ID column), even if they're useless. Permutation importance on held-out data is more reliable — though it spreads credit unpredictably between correlated features."),
 (13, 2, "Is the tree a decision tree learns the best possible tree?",
  "Assuming training finds the optimum.",
  "No. It's built greedily, choosing the best split at each step without looking ahead. Finding the truly optimal tree is computationally intractable in general."),
 (13, 1, "Why do trees draw \"staircase\" boundaries for a diagonal pattern?",
  "Not noticing the shape.",
  "Each split is on one feature at a time, so boundaries are axis-aligned. A diagonal boundary needs many small steps. A linear model or a rotated feature (such as $x_1 - x_2$) captures it with one split."),

 (14, 2, "Does bagging reduce bias or variance? And boosting?",
  "\"Both reduce error\" without saying how.",
  "Bagging (random forests) mainly reduces <em>variance</em> by averaging many deep, overfit trees. Boosting mainly reduces <em>bias</em> by adding simple models that correct remaining errors."),
 (14, 2, "Will a random forest overfit if you add more trees?",
  "Treating trees like boosting rounds.",
  "No. More trees make the average more stable; the test error levels off, it doesn't rise. Gradient boosting is different: more rounds <em>can</em> overfit, so use early stopping."),
 (14, 3, "Why does a random forest choose a random subset of features at each split?",
  "\"For speed.\"",
  "To decorrelate the trees. The variance of an average of $B$ trees is $\\rho\\sigma^2 + \\frac{1-\\rho}{B}\\sigma^2$, where $\\rho$ is their correlation. With $\\rho = 0.5$ and 100 trees it's 0.505σ²; with $\\rho = 0.1$ it's 0.109σ². Bootstrap samples alone leave trees too similar, because they all split on the strongest feature."),
 (14, 2, "What is the out-of-bag score?",
  "Not knowing about it.",
  "Each tree skips about 37% of the examples. Predicting each example using only the trees that didn't see it gives a free validation estimate, without a separate hold-out set."),
 (14, 2, "Why is boosting sensitive to label noise?",
  "Thinking all ensembles are robust.",
  "Boosting keeps up-weighting the examples it gets wrong. A mislabelled example is always \"wrong\", so it attracts more and more attention. Bagging doesn't do this."),
 (14, 2, "On tabular data, why do gradient-boosted trees often beat deep neural networks?",
  "Assuming deep learning always wins.",
  "Tabular features are heterogeneous (mixed scales, categories, missing values) and lack the spatial or sequential structure that CNNs and Transformers exploit. Trees handle these naturally and need little preprocessing; benchmarks repeatedly find tuned GBDTs (XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost) very hard to beat on typical tables."),
 (14, 2, "How do the learning rate and number of trees interact in gradient boosting?",
  "Tuning them separately.",
  "Smaller learning rates need more trees but usually generalise better. A common recipe: fix a small learning rate (e.g. 0.05), then let early stopping on a validation set choose the number of trees."),

 (15, 2, "Which training points determine an SVM's boundary?",
  "\"All of them.\"",
  "Only the support vectors: points on or inside the margin, or misclassified. Points safely beyond the margin have zero hinge loss and could be deleted without changing the model."),
 (15, 2, "What happens as you make C very large in a soft-margin SVM?",
  "Thinking larger C means more regularisation.",
  "The opposite: C is the penalty for margin violations, so a large C tolerates almost none — a narrow margin that fits the training data closely and can overfit. A small C allows more violations and a wider, simpler margin. (C is like $1/\\lambda$.)"),
 (15, 2, "What does the RBF kernel's gamma control?",
  "Confusing it with C.",
  "How far each training point's influence reaches. Large gamma: very local influence, wiggly boundaries, overfitting. Small gamma: smooth boundaries, possibly underfitting. Tune C and gamma together with cross-validation."),
 (15, 2, "Does the kernel trick compute the high-dimensional features?",
  "Thinking it transforms the data explicitly.",
  "No — that's the point. The kernel gives the dot product in the feature space directly from the original vectors. For 100 features and a degree-3 polynomial kernel, the feature space has 171,700 dimensions, yet each kernel value costs one 100-term dot product."),
 (15, 2, "Why are kernel SVMs rarely used on millions of examples?",
  "Assuming they scale like linear models.",
  "Training compares pairs of examples, so time and memory grow roughly between $n^2$ and $n^3$. Use linear SVMs, approximate kernels, or gradient-boosted trees for big data."),
 (15, 1, "Do SVMs need feature scaling?",
  "Treating them like trees.",
  "Yes. The margin and the RBF kernel are distance-based, so features with large ranges dominate."),

 (16, 2, "What shapes of cluster does k-means find well, and which does it fail on?",
  "Thinking k-means finds any group.",
  "It works best for compact, roughly spherical clusters of similar size, because it assigns each point to the nearest centre. It fails on elongated, ring-shaped or very unequal clusters — try DBSCAN, Gaussian mixtures or spectral clustering."),
 (16, 2, "You run k-means twice and get different clusters. Is it broken?",
  "Expecting a deterministic answer.",
  "No. It converges to a local optimum that depends on the starting centroids. Use k-means++ initialisation, several restarts (<code>n_init</code>), and keep the run with the lowest inertia. Fix the random seed for reproducibility."),
 (16, 2, "Why can't you choose $k$ by minimising inertia?",
  "Picking the $k$ with the lowest inertia.",
  "Inertia always falls as $k$ grows and reaches 0 when every point is its own cluster. Look for an \"elbow\", use the silhouette score, or — best — ask which grouping is useful for the purpose."),
 (16, 2, "How do you evaluate clustering when there are no labels?",
  "Saying \"you can't\".",
  "Internal measures (silhouette, Davies–Bouldin), stability across resamples and random seeds, and above all usefulness: are the clusters interpretable and do they lead to different actions? If some labels exist, compare with them (adjusted Rand index)."),
 (16, 2, "Can you use k-means on categorical data?",
  "One-hot encoding and running it anyway.",
  "Means and Euclidean distances aren't meaningful for categories. Use k-modes (or k-prototypes for mixed data), or a distance designed for categories."),
 (16, 2, "What does DBSCAN need instead of $k$, and what does it do with outliers?",
  "Thinking it needs no settings.",
  "A neighbourhood radius (eps) and a minimum number of points to form a dense region. It finds any number of clusters of any shape and labels points in sparse regions as noise rather than forcing them into a cluster."),

 (17, 2, "Should you standardise before PCA?",
  "Running PCA on raw features.",
  "Almost always. PCA chases variance, so a feature in large units dominates. In Lesson 17, measuring one score ×10 made PC1 keep 99.8% of the variance and point almost entirely along that feature."),
 (17, 3, "PCA keeps 95% of the variance. Can it still remove the feature that best predicts the target?",
  "Assuming high variance means useful.",
  "Yes. PCA ignores the target. A low-variance direction can be the most predictive one, and PCA might discard it. If prediction is the goal, compare with supervised selection, or tune the number of components by cross-validated performance."),
 (17, 2, "You fit PCA on the full dataset, then split into train and test. Problem?",
  "Thinking unsupervised steps can't leak.",
  "Yes, a mild leak: the components were shaped by the test data. Fit PCA on the training data only (inside a Pipeline) and apply that transformation to the test data."),
 (17, 2, "In a t-SNE plot, two clusters are far apart. Are they very different?",
  "Reading t-SNE like a map.",
  "Not necessarily. t-SNE preserves local neighbourhoods, not global distances or cluster sizes, and its picture changes with the perplexity setting. Use it to explore, not to measure."),
 (17, 2, "What's the difference between PCA and feature selection?",
  "Using the terms interchangeably.",
  "Feature selection keeps a subset of the original features (still interpretable). PCA creates new features that mix all the originals (often harder to interpret)."),
 (17, 3, "How are PCA and the SVD related?",
  "Thinking PCA requires the covariance matrix.",
  "For centred data $X = U S V^\\top$, the columns of $V$ are the principal directions and the variances are $s_i^2/(n-1)$. Libraries compute PCA via the SVD because it's more numerically stable than forming $X^\\top X$."),
]
